        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>
<p> Although humans have been flying in space for over fifty years, a relatively new medical phenomenon has been found to occur with longer duration flights, and that is the degradation of visual acuity. Approximately 29% of astronauts on short-term (~2 wk) space shuttle flights and 60% on long-duration (~6 mo) missions to the International Space Station (ISS) are reported to have experienced some impairment in distant or near visual acuity, and with the long-term flights the visual degradation in several astronauts has not resolved over time upon return to Earth (1). Though data are limited, overall findings from 10 long-duration astronauts who experienced in-flight and post-flight complications demonstrate symptoms of optic disc edema, globe flattening, choroidal folds, cotton wool spots, nerve fiber layer thickening, and elevated intracranial pressure. These visual disturbances have been hypothesized to be related to increases in intracranial pressure (ICP) (1-3). Various factors have been suggested to account for the elevations in ICP during spaceflight, including alterations in cerebral artery structure, mechanics and function. In addition, modeling studies have shown that a compromise in the integrity of the vascular blood-brain barrier would serve to elevate ICP. While much attention has been directed toward the role of the cerebral vasculature in elevating ICP, little work has been done to examine conditions of the vasculature in the eye and the potential role of microgravity in altering the blood-retinal barrier, which maintains a similar function in the eye for regulating intraocular pressure as the blood-brain barrier does in the cranium. One condition known to compromise the blood-retinal barrier is oxidative stress. For example, in diabetic retinopathy, the leading cause of blindness in Western society, elevations in oxidative stress compromise the blood-retinal barrier and increase vascular permeability in the eye. Therefore, the purpose of this biospecimen request is to address the following specific aims:

<p>Aim 1. To determine whether spaceflight on the ISS increases levels of 4-hydroxynonenal (4-HNE) in the retina, a marker of lipid peroxidation due to oxidative stress, and up-regulates genes involved in the vascular endothelial cell response to oxidative stress and extracellular matrix remodeling in the eye. Like that found in the eyes from mice flown for 13 d on the STS-135 Space Shuttle mission, it is hypothesized that long-term spaceflight will increase levels of 4-HNE in the retina and that multiple genes involved in the regulation of oxidative stress will likewise be up-regulated.

<p>Aim 2. To determine whether markers of oxidative stress in the eye differ in RR-1 mice that are euthanized and frozen on the ISS versus mice where the eyes are harvested and frozen following live return from the ISS via splashdown in the Pacific Ocean and naval transport to the California coast. Levels of retinal 4-HNE and expression of genes involved in vascular endothelial cell response to oxidative stress and extracellular matrix remodeling will be compared in the eyes obtained from the RR-1 mice and eyes obtained from mice as part of the JAXA-NASA Tissue Sharing for Rodent Research agreement. This latter research project, entitled, “Mouse Epigenetics,” with Satoru Takahashi, M.D., Ph.D., and Michael Delp, Ph.D., serving as the JAXA and NASA Principal Investigators, respectively, will provide Michael Delp with the eyes from these live return mice after spending 30 days on the ISS. Comparison of the responses in the eyes from these two groups of ISS mice will allow investigators to estimate the impact of the live return on oxidative stress in the eyes. It is hypothesized that the live return and the conditions associated with obtaining live animals back on Earth will exacerbate markers of oxidative stress in the eyes.

APPROACH:<br>
<p>Change in expression of genes involved in cellular functions of mitochondrial biology, endothelial cell biology and oxidative stress will be examined in the eyes from each of the four groups. RNA extraction from whole eye and quantitative reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) array services will be performed at Qiagen service core (Frederick, MD).

<p>PCR reactions will be performed to evaluate expression of 84 genes/array using RT2 ProliferTM PCR array PAMM-15A (Mouse Endothelial Cell Biology PCR Array), and PAMM-065 (Mouse Oxidative Stress PCR Array). Data analysis will be performed using Qiagen’ PCR array data analysis software. Relative changes in gene expression will be calculated using the comparative threshold cycle (Delta Delta Ct, DDCt) method. This method first subtracts the Ct (threshold cycle number) of the gene-average Ct of the 5 housekeeping genes on the array (Gusb, Hprt, Hsp90ab1, Gapdh and Actb) to normalize for the amount of RNA per sample. Finally the DDCt will be calculated: normalized average Ct of the flight group – normalized average Ct of the AEM control group. This DDCt value will be raised to the power of 2 to calculate the degree of change. Whole eyes from five mice in each group will be evaluated individually.</p>

RESULTS:<br>
<p>This experiment is currently in progress.  Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
